  "query_preview": "..High pressure is centered off the southern\nCalifornia coast his morning, bringing a dry northwest flow to\nUtah and southwest Wyoming. Seeing some high clouds from a \nPacific Northwest storm system passing well to the north of the \narea, but otherwise conditions are tranquil with highs expected to\nbe near seasonal normals once again.\n\nThe ridge is expected to flatten during the day across the area\ntomorrow as a strong Pacific Northwest storm system approaches,\nbringing an increasing westerly flow, allowing for better mixing\nin currently inverted valleys and warmer temperatures, with maxes\nrunning 5-10F above seasonal normals. This trough is eventually\nexpected to cross northern Utah and southwest Wyoming Friday night\ninto Saturday morning, bringing light valley rain/mountain snow to\nnorthern portions of the area, primarily north of I-80. Overall,\nguidance is trending northward with the track of this system, thus\nlimiting the southern extent of this precipitation. \n\nSaturday's maxes should return to near normal for this time of\nyear, but rebuilding high pressure will bring increasingly mild\ntemperatures for the early part of the upcoming week. Guidance\ncontinues to indicate the potential of a more significant storm\nsystem to impact the area for the middle to latter part of the\nupcoming week, but a large subset of the guidance is indicating\nthis might be an event primarily for southern Utah. Still,\nconfidence in any solution remains low this far out.",
